From: Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com>
Subject: [PATCH] libtiff: migrate legacy typedefs to C99 standard types

libtiff 4.5+ explicitly marks its legacy `uint8` / `uint16` / `uint32`
typedefs as `__attribute__((deprecated))` (see /usr/include/tiff.h
in Fedora 44+). Combined with O3DE's `-Werror`, every use of those
typedefs becomes a hard build failure:

    error: 'uint32' is deprecated [-Werror,-Wdeprecated-declarations]
    error: 'uint8' is deprecated [-Werror,-Wdeprecated-declarations]

O3DE has two consumers of `<tiffio.h>` that use the legacy names
(a third — `Gems/Atom/Feature/Common/.../FrameCaptureSystemComponent.cpp`
— is already on the standard `uint32_t` family and needs no change):

  1. `Gems/Atom/Asset/ImageProcessingAtom/.../TIFFLoader.cpp` —
     the modern Atom-pipeline TIFF loader. 9 legacy `uint32` uses.
     Adjacent code in the same file already uses `uint32_t` (line 371's
     outer imageY loop) — partial migration upstream; this finishes it.
  2. `Code/Editor/Util/ImageTIF.cpp` — the legacy Editor TIFF loader.
     35 legacy uses across `uint32`, `uint16`, `uint8`. All in
     local-variable declarations, casts, and template arguments.

Mechanical replacement: every `uint8`/`uint16`/`uint32` token (NOT
already `_t`-suffixed) becomes its standard C99 equivalent. Same
underlying types on all platforms; behavior unchanged.

Apply unconditionally — works against both the bundled libtiff
(older revision, still defines both names) and Fedora's libtiff 4.5+.
The patch keeps the source tree consistent regardless of whether
libtiff resolves from the upstream CDN bundle or from system
tiff-devel.

Required to unblock the `--with system_tiff` Stage 1 swap; also
benefits bundled-libtiff builds as the bundle ages and starts
shipping the C99 names.

Upstream-worthy: yes, as a standalone PR. The migration is required
for any build against modern libtiff regardless of distro packaging
(Fedora 44+, Ubuntu 24.04+, Arch, etc.) and the change is
conservative (mechanical rename to standard types). Same file
already started the migration — this finishes it.

Path-anchored at the two specific files so future upstream rebases
that move adjacent code (different line numbers) but keep the files
in place still apply.

Signed-off-by: Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com>
